{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481944,"nonce":"MoorNFWDIZVZDInrqCdSuUAP8fE=","title":"**What is wrong.** SPEC §E.1 states one rule — *\"`limit` default 50, max 200\"* — and the tree holds five copies of the pair and six implementations of the clamp. Four of the clamps are byte-identical.","message":"**The demonstration.** The constants:\n\n- `internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:25`, `:27` — `DefaultLimit = 50`, `MaxLimit = 200`\n- `internal/grouping/repository/mapper.go:22`, `:24` — same names, same values\n- `internal/sources/repository/errors.go:20`, `:22` — same\n- `internal/channels/repository/errors.go:19`, `:21` — same\n- `internal/platform/httpx/cursor.go:22`, `:24` — the same two numbers under different names, `DefaultPageLimit` / `MaxPageLimit`\n\nThe clamp, byte-identical in four files (`internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:39`, `internal/grouping/repository/mapper.go:27`, `internal/sources/repository/errors.go:25`, `internal/channels/repository/errors.go:24`):\n\n```go\nfunc clampLimit(n int) int {\n\tswitch {\n\tcase n \u003c= 0:\n\t\treturn DefaultLimit\n\tcase n \u003e MaxLimit:\n\t\treturn MaxLimit\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn n\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n`platform/httpx` then writes the same switch twice more against its own pair of names — `Params.Limit` at `internal/platform/httpx/bind.go:270-280` and `Keyset` at `internal/platform/httpx/cursor.go:82-90`. Between them the four repository copies are called from 18 production sites across `alerts`, `grouping`, `sources` and `channels`.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the exact shape of the defect ADR 0026 already paid for once, and CONTEXT.md §5 rule 2c records what it cost: *\"They used to keep copies with a ⚠️ comment each, ADR 0026 moved three defaults at once and two copies were missed, and the miss is silent\"*. The fix there was to make the compiler the enforcement: *\"Every declaration outside this package is now a REFERENCE to it, which the compiler keeps honest\"*. The page bounds got no such treatment, so a §E.1 amendment lands in whichever of the five homes the author happens to open, and the four that shipped `50`/`200` today will disagree tomorrow with no compile error and no test failure.\n\nThere is one honest caveat on placement. CONTEXT.md §5 rule 2c is explicit that `platform/tuning` is *\"not a general dumping ground\"* and is *\"constants and nothing else — no types, no behaviour, no import but `time`\"* — so `clampLimit`, being behaviour, does not belong there. `platform/httpx` already owns the numbers and one of the clamps, and `platform/db` already owns `Keyset`, which is what the repositories clamp *for*; either is a legitimate home under rule 7. `httpx` is the poorer of the two here, because a repository importing an HTTP package to bound a query is a layering smell even where no rule forbids it.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/repository/mapper.go:23-48`, `internal/grouping/repository/mapper.go:19-36`, `internal/sources/repository/errors.go:17-34`, `internal/channels/repository/errors.go:16-33`, `internal/platform/httpx/cursor.go:20-25` and `:82-90`, `internal/platform/httpx/bind.go:267-280`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `DefaultLimit`/`MaxLimit` exist in exactly one package — `platform/db` for preference, since `db.Keyset` is what they bound — the four repository `clampLimit` functions and the two `httpx` clamps all call one exported helper, and the only remaining declarations of `50` and `200` are that package's.","files":null}]}